[16.244782, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2;37m  … the founder agent is working (about 40s live, sped up in this recording)\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[56.414766, "o", "Here's the founder agent's plan. Its per-stage timings and costs are typical industry ranges, not         \r\nmeasurements from your stack.                                                                             \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mGoal this week:\u001b[0m get more calls to end in a confirmed booking. Fix latency first, since callers hang up on \r\nslow replies. Cost isn't your blocker yet.                                                                \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mFounder A: latency (Mon–Thu)\u001b[0m                                                                              \r\n\r\n\u001b[36m 1 \u001b[0m\u001b[1mMeasure first (Mon–Tue).\u001b[0m Log five timestamps per turn: caller stops talking, final transcript, first   \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0mLLM token, first speech audio, first audio reaching the caller. Do this on 50 real calls. A typical    \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m2.5s splits into roughly 0.6–0.9s waiting to decide the caller has finished, 0.6–1.0s for the LLM,     \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m0.2–0.4s for text-to-speech and 0.2–0.4s for the network.                                              \r\n\u001b[36m 2 \u001b[0m\u001b[1mFix in this order (Wed–Thu):\u001b[0m                                                                           \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mWait only 300–400ms of silence before replying, using smart turn detection so the agent doesn't cut \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mcallers off.                                                                                        \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mStream every stage, and send the first sentence to speech before the LLM finishes.                  \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mUse a small, fast model for routine turns.                                                          \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mCut the prompt to under 2k tokens and look up clinic data with a tool call instead of putting it in \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m   \u001b[0mthe prompt.                                                                                         \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mPre-record the greeting and confirmations.                                                          \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mRun all vendors in the same region as your phone provider.                                          \r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m • \u001b[0mPlay a short filler (\"Let me check…\") during availability lookups.                                  \r\n\u001b[36m 3 \u001b[0m\u001b[1mTarget:\u001b[0m under 1s for a typical reply and under 1.5s worst case. Expect about 1.0–1.2s by Friday without\r\n\u001b[36m   \u001b[0mchanging your architecture.                                                                            \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCost: $0.40/min now, target $0.10–0.15/min, only where it overlaps with the latency work.\u001b[0m Speech output   \r\nusually costs the most. Switch to a cheaper fast voice, use the same small model, turn on prompt caching, \r\ntrim conversation history and aim for calls under 3 minutes. At $0.40/min a 3-minute booking costs about  \r\n$1.20, against a new patient worth $500 or more.                                                          \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mNot this week:\u001b[0m self-hosting models, rebuilding the stack, fine-tuning, or negotiating vendor contracts.   \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mFounder B: what's really blocking you.\u001b[0m The bigger problem is likely failed bookings rather than speed:    \r\nmisheard names and dates of birth, callers repeating themselves, or bookings that never reach the clinic's\r\nscheduling software. Listen to 30 calls and tag why each failed (by Wed), work toward 2–3 paid pilots, and\r\nask clinics what they'd pay per booking or per month.                                                     \r\n\r\n\u001b[1mThe one metric to watch:\u001b[0m booking completion rate. That's the share of callers wanting an appointment who  \r\nend up with a confirmed slot in the clinic's system, with no staff fix needed. Re-measure it, plus latency\r\nand cost per minute, on Friday.                                                                           \r\n"]
